-	<!-- custom: based on https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/La_T%C3%A8ne_culture + GPT-5.2-Thinking blurb thanks -->
+	<!-- custom: based on https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/La_T%C3%A8ne_culture + AI-generated blurb with the help of GPT-5.2-Thinking thanks -->
 	<TEXT>
 		<Tag>TXT_KEY_BUILDING_CELTIC_LA_TENE_SMITHY_PEDIA</Tag>
 		<English>[TAB]The La T�ne culture was a European Iron Age culture. It developed and flourished during the late Iron Age (from about 450 BC to the Roman conquest in the 1st century BC), succeeding the early Iron Age Hallstatt culture without any definite cultural break, under considerable Mediterranean influence from the Greeks in pre-Roman Gaul, the Etruscans, and the Golasecca culture, but whose artistic style nevertheless did not depend on those Mediterranean influences.[PARAGRAPH:1]La T�ne culture's territorial extent corresponded to what is now France, Belgium, Switzerland, Austria, England, Southern Germany, the Czech Republic, Northern Italy and Central Italy, Slovenia, Hungary and Liechtenstein, as well as adjacent parts of the Netherlands, Slovakia, Serbia, Croatia, Transylvania (western Romania), and Transcarpathia (western Ukraine). The Celtiberians of western Iberia shared many aspects of the culture, though not generally the artistic style. To the north extended the contemporary Pre-Roman Iron Age of Northern Europe, including the Jastorf culture of Northern Germany and Denmark and all the way to Galatia in Asia Minor (today Turkey).[PARAGRAPH:1]Centered on ancient Gaul, the culture became very widespread, and encompasses a wide variety of local differences. It is often distinguished from earlier and neighbouring cultures mainly by the La T�ne style of Celtic art, characterized by curving "swirly" decoration, especially of metalwork.[PARAGRAPH:1]It is named after the type site of La T�ne on the north side of Lake Neuch�tel in Switzerland, where thousands of objects had been deposited in the lake, as was discovered after the water level dropped in 1857 (due to the Jura water correction).[PARAGRAPH:1]In the popular understanding, La T�ne describes the culture and art of the ancient Celts, a term that is firmly entrenched in the popular understanding, but it is considered controversial by modern scholarship.[PARAGRAPH:2]In the La T�ne world, metalwork was a signature craft, with bronze, iron and gold objects made in the distinctive La T�ne style of curving decoration, spirals and interlace. Archaeological finds include elite jewelry such as torcs and fibulae, but also helmets, shields, and horse trappings, showing that smithing mattered for both prestige and war. A smithy is the working shop of a blacksmith, where heated metal is forged on an anvil and shaped into tools, fittings, and weapons.[PARAGRAPH:1]A La T�ne Smithy represents the skilled labor and workshop tradition behind this culture: turning raw metal into useful equipment and visible status objects. In game terms, access to copper and iron supports the smithy with steady supply, repairs, and replacement gear, which can translate into stronger morale and confidence.</English>
 	</TEXT>
+	<!-- custom: AI-generated with the help of GPT-5.2-Thinking thanks -->
+	<TEXT>
+		<Tag>TXT_KEY_BUILDING_ETHIOPIAN_METSEHAF_BET_PEDIA</Tag>
+		<English>[TAB]Traditional education in the Ethiopian Orthodox world is organized as a ladder of church schools. Students begin in the Nebab Bet (house of reading), learning to read and memorizing core prayers and the Psalms. Advanced tracks include liturgy, chant, and poetry, and culminate in the Metsehaf Bet, a school centered on deep textual interpretation.[PARAGRAPH:1]Metsehaf Bet is described as a general term for a school of commentaries with four branches: Old Testament (Beluy), New Testament (Haddis), Church Fathers and related studies (Liqawent, including canon law and calendar calculation), and monastic literature (Menekosat). Instruction emphasizes close reading where lines of a text are interpreted and theological, moral, and historical questions are developed from the commentary tradition.</English>
+	</TEXT>
 </Civ4GameText>

